 ##### Note about time zones in JS
 
-By default, there's only one time zone available in Kotlin/JS: the `SYSTEM` time zone with a fixed offset.
+By default, Kotlin/JS makes only two time zones available: `TimeZone.UTC`, and the host's own time zone,
+whose `id` is `SYSTEM`. `TimeZoneContext.System.availableZoneIds()` returns just `UTC`, and asking for any
+region-based zone such as `Europe/Berlin` throws `IllegalTimeZoneException`.
+The `SYSTEM` zone is not a fixed-offset zone: it derives the offset from the JavaScript `Date` object for
+each instant separately, so it follows whatever daylight saving rules the host is configured with.
+What it cannot do is answer for any zone other than the host's own.
 
 If you want to use all time zones in the Kotlin/JS platform, you need to add the following npm dependency
 (replace X.X.X below with the latest version [from here](https://www.npmjs.com/package/@js-joda/timezone?activeTab=versions)):
